# Retain actual acetic fermentation; do not substitute an infused vinegar.
r=add(95,'Fermented Carrot Vinegar (Modern Juice Method)',60,80640,'Carrot juice is fermented with a live vinegar culture and measured drinking alcohol to make a tangy cooking vinegar.',[I('juice',750,'ml','pasteurized 100% carrot juice, without preservatives','Use the same volume of pasteurized juice from another carrot variety; do not substitute a juice blend.'),I('starter',250,'ml','unpasteurized cider vinegar labeled 5% acidity, containing a live vinegar mother','Use the same volume of a live vinegar starter already at 5% acetic acidity; pasteurized vinegar will not supply the culture.'),I('vodka',200,'ml','plain drinking vodka at exactly 40% alcohol by volume','Use another neutral drinking spirit at exactly 40% ABV. Do not use cleaning alcohol, denatured alcohol or a different strength without recalculating the formula.')],['This recipe requires a food thermometer, a calibrated food pH meter and a vinegar acidity titration kit that reports percent acetic acid, not a wine kit reporting tartaric acid. Wash a wide-mouth 2-liter glass jar and utensils thoroughly, rinse and sanitize with a food-contact brewing sanitizer according to its label. Keep test chemicals separate from all food.','Bring {{juice}} to room temperature in the clean jar. Stir in {{starter}} with its mother and {{vodka}}. This volume formula begins at approximately 6.7% alcohol by volume. Measure and record the initial pH and titratable acidity using separate samples. The pH must be 4.0 or lower before warm fermentation; if it is higher, do not proceed with this batch. Discard tested samples, never returning them to the food.','Cover the jar with a clean, tightly woven cloth or paper coffee filter secured around the rim, allowing air in while excluding insects. Do not fit an airtight lid. Keep out of sunlight at 22–26°C (72–79°F). Stir gently once daily with a clean utensil to supply oxygen, then replace the cover.','Allow about 4–8 weeks for acetic fermentation. Check weekly with the pH meter and vinegar acidity kit, following the kit’s sample size and calculation exactly. Keep a dated record. The pH should remain at or below 4.0 and acidity should rise above its starting value. Discard the whole batch without tasting if fuzzy mold, colored growth, a putrid odor or a pH above 4.0 develops. A smooth pale vinegar-mother film is expected.','Finish when titratable acidity reaches 5–7% expressed as acetic acid and no longer rises over two measurements taken several days apart. A sour taste or a low pH alone does not establish this endpoint. If acidity has not increased after 2 weeks, or the endpoint is not reached by 8 weeks, discard and restart with a fresh live starter instead of extending an inactive batch.','Strain the finished vinegar through a clean coffee filter, discarding the mother and sediment. Warm the strained vinegar in a stainless-steel pan to 65°C (149°F), checking throughout the liquid with a clean thermometer. Pour into clean, heat-safe bottles, cool promptly and refrigerate at 4°C (40°F) or colder.','Label with the date and “homemade carrot vinegar—refrigerate; not for canning.” Use within 1 month in cooked sauces or dressings. This home acidity check does not certify it for food preservation or establish that all alcohol is gone. Do not use it as the acid source for canned or room-temperature-stored foods.'],'The original industrial carrot-pulp process is replaced explicitly with a modern carrot-juice acetic fermentation, not a vinegar infusion. Measured vodka supplies the alcohol that the sugar-depleted historical pulp leaves uncertain; the live vinegar supplies culture and starting acid. The 750:250:200 ml formulation, working pH ceiling and completion checks are disclosed modern choices. The original scan actually gives wine tartar, tartaric acid and isinglass, not cream of tartar and egg white; those uncontrolled additions are replaced by measured culture and filtration. The estimated eight-week total includes fermentation, with about 30 minutes active preparation plus brief daily checks. About 900 ml finished yield is an estimate after evaporation, sampling and filtering. This method has not been fermentation-tested by SavorShelf and is not alcohol-free.','Condiments')
r['notes']=r['notes'].replace('Cool leftovers promptly in shallow containers and refrigerate within 2 hours at 4°C (40°F) or colder.','During fermentation follow the dedicated temperature instructions; refrigerate the bottled vinegar as directed.')
